\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Job Openings Rise as Layoffs Fall Slightly\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bryan Mena \par \uc2 743 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 August 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. job openings rose in July as employers scooped up workers in a tight labor market.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department on Tuesday said there were a seasonally adjusted 11.2 million job openings in July, up from the previous month's upwardly revised 11 million. Job openings have remained elevated and above 10 million since the summer of 2021.\par \par \uc2 The number of times workers quit their jobs edged down to 4.2 million in July from June's 4.3 million. Layoffs and discharges fell slightly to 1.4 million in July from the prior month's level. Hiring slowed slightly to 6.4 million from 6.5 million in June.\par \par \uc2 The jobs market remains strong, but some signs have pointed to slowing momentum as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raises interest rates to tamp down inflation. The average pace of job growth in the first half of the year was slower than in all of 2021, and new applications for unemployment benefits have hovered near the highest point of the year in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 A separate report released Tuesday said consumer confidence improved in August, ending a three-month streak of declines. Private-research group the Conference Board said Tuesday that its consumer-confidence index rose to 103.2 in August from a revised 95.3 in July, as households' concerns about inflation retreated amid lower gasoline prices.\par \par \uc2 Later this week, the Labor Department will report how many jobs the U.S. economy added in August and say whether the unemployment rate held at the 50-year low reached in July. The August employment report will include updated figures on wage gains, a factor driving high inflation.\par \par \uc2 Job openings still greatly exceed the number of unemployed people seeking work, and separate private-sector estimates show that labor demand remains high, but is slowing as the broader economy loses steam.\par \par \uc2 Jobs site \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2  estimates there were about 10 million job openings through mid-August, down from the seasonally adjusted 11.2 million openings in July.\par \par \uc2 Industries where work is more likely to be done in person, like tourism and education, had some of the highest job-opening rates in July. The South, the Midwest and the West all had a higher job openings rate in July than they did a year before.\par \par \uc2 "While the labor market is still carrying solid momentum, we expect the hefty pace of job growth will moderate in the second half of the year as companies face a weaker domestic and external backdrop," \uc2 Lydia Boussour\uc2 , lead U.S. economist at Oxford Economics, wrote in an analyst note.\par \par \uc2 The high level of job openings means that it remains easy for laid-off workers to quickly land a new job. However, \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2  data show that some job seekers have become more concerned about job security and more are worried that a job offer they received might be rescinded, according to \uc2 Julia Pollak\uc2 , chief economist of \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The share of job seekers who have said they felt financial pressure to accept the first offer they received has also increased, she added.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Pollak said she expects the number of times workers quit their jobs, which reached 4.2 million in July, to remain elevated as a result of the impact of remote work. Quitting picked up in 2021 as job openings increased.\par \par \uc2 "Any person looking for a job before might have been limited to the three job openings in their neighborhood, but now they can apply for 3,000 remote job openings within their occupation nationwide," Ms. Pollak said.\par \par \uc2 While the U.S. economy in July had recouped the 22 million jobs it lost at the start of the Covid-19 pandemic, the labor force remains smaller than it was in the beginning of 2020. The labor-force participation rate -- the share of the population 16 and older working or seeking work -- also fell to 62.1% in July, below the 63.4% in February 2020.\par \par \uc2 George Pfeffer\uc2 , a management committee member at \uc2 DPR Construction\uc2 , said hiring hasn't slowed down as the company of roughly 10,000 workers tries to fill about 300 vacancies.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pfeffer added that his company will likely hesitate to lay off workers even as economic growth slows.\par \par \uc2 "Keeping our workforce employed for when things turn back up is way more valuable than laying them off and trying to find them again later," Mr. Pfeffer said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220831ei8v0000y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
