\par\par \b \uc2 Strike the Jeans and Flannels -- Labor Activists Get Fashionable --- Union leaders attract attention for their style, but veterans are unimpressed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Herrera \par \uc2 985 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 John Elward, a truck driver for \uc2 United Parcel Service Inc\uc2 . near San Francisco, collects labor union memorabilia. He owns at least 12 \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  jackets, his favorite being a green one labeled "Irish American." Then there are his dozens of union pins and patches.\par \par \uc2 So when organizers in April became the first to unionize an \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . facility, the 42-year-old Mr. Elward grew excited. Several of the leaders seemed to have a flair for fashion -- not something typically associated with labor activism.\par \par \uc2 Chris Smalls, the activist who led the organizing drive at Amazon, wore a red baseball cap, red sweats and hoodie and a red "Amazon Labor Union" shirt on top that day, all finished with a pair of oversize sunglasses. Pictures of his outfit went viral.\par \par \uc2 At the Time 100 gala two months later, Mr. Smalls went with black overalls and a black blazer, along with a bandanna and huge black shades. For an appearance on "The Daily Show," he donned a bubble gum-pink Amazon Labor Union shirt and a printed baby blue bomber jacket.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Elward, whose grandfather once headed a local branch of the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 , had already noticed more style creeping into labor activism. He launched the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account "Dripped Out Trade Unionists" -- using hip-hop slang for ultrafashionable -- to chronicle the sartorial leanings of retail employees, machine operators, engineers and other workers.\par \par \uc2 He now has about 30,000 followers and regularly gets messages from workers sending him snapshots of their outfits or asking things such as, "Where can I get that jacket?"\par \par \uc2 Some others, though, would be happy to see things stay on the plainer side. Matt Lelou, a factory worker and president of a \uc2 Communications Workers of America\uc2  local in Minnesota, is strictly back to basics -- jeans and work boots -- when he takes part in union activities.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not the guy into fashion," he said. "I'm just focused on the nuts and bolts."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lelou, 46, cautions workers publicly protesting pay and working conditions not to get too snazzy. "If you're wearing $400 shoes," he said, "that could shoot some of your arguments in the foot."\par \par \uc2 Style doesn't usually spring to mind as an element of the modern American labor movement. Striking factory or mine workers are often pictured in flannels or work clothes.\par \par \uc2 But the pandemic put essential workers, including nurses and delivery drivers, in the public eye -- and along the way made their gear trendy. Also, young people who generally have a sense of style have often led the union movements of the last two years.\par \par \uc2 In England, several fashion brands launched shirts inspired by the \uc2 National Health Service\uc2 . Late last year, the \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  teamed up with shoe company Vans on a footwear and apparel collection. It featured an iteration of Vans' popular Sk8-Hi shoes decorated with the postal service's eagle logo. There was also a Vans/USPS-themed pocket T-shirt and beanie.\par \par \uc2 Sarina Daniels, a 28-year-old in Fort Collins, Colo., who describes herself as a fan of the postal service, bought both the high-top and low-top variations of the shoe. "My carriers have always been great, and as a suburban kid, we knew our mailman, and mail was sort of a staple of my childhood," said Ms. Daniels, who works as a bookseller.\par \par \uc2 She was so high on the postal-theme sneakers that she tried to get a pair for her local mail carrier, but they sold out before she could.\par \par \uc2 Some street-style casual wear now incorporates labor-union T-shirts, worn along with bucket hats, studded-out baseball caps or varsity jackets. In May, Mr. Elward tweeted a photo of actress Susan Sarandon in a \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  bomber jacket.\par \par \uc2 "Love my @Teamsters jackets," she responded.\par \par \uc2 Bob Reiter\uc2 , president of the \uc2 Chicago Federation of Labor\uc2 , which has about 300 affiliated unions, has been trying to modernize that organization's merchandise to keep up.\par \par \uc2 "We have asked, 'How do we sell stuff to get the message out, but that our activists want to wear?'" he said. Mr. Reiter, 48, studies the merchandise tables at music concerts for inspiration. "A lot of those styles are where the cutting edge is," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Reiter recently led a website update to offer goods in line with the latest fashions. It includes trucker hats, a black shirt with "#union" on it and, soon, bomber jackets emblazoned with the union's initials.\par \par \uc2 Angelika Maldonado, a 28-year-old organizer with the Amazon Labor Union, sometimes wears all-black outfits, including a black ALU shirt and black \uc2 Nike\uc2  Air Max shoes. Other days, it's a pink ALU shirt and biker shorts.\par \par \uc2 At a Baltimore-area \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 . retail store that in June became the first to unionize, workers have started a "\uc2 Crocs\uc2  Sunday" tradition, said employee Julian Milligan. The colorful clogs are back in vogue.\par \par \uc2 As their organizing effort took off, some employees began to integrate their union shirts with their wardrobes. "Everyone in the store is pretty fashionable," said Mr. Milligan, 24, who said he has more than 10 variations of \uc2 Crocs\uc2 . "I try to have a different pair of \uc2 Crocs\uc2  each Sunday."\par \par \uc2 The fashion fuss is lost on Lewis Overlock, a \uc2 Laborers' International Union of North America\uc2  officer and former construction worker in Maine.\par \par \uc2 "I don't need a walk-in closet," said Mr. Overlock, 47. To hold his clothes, "if you just give me a barrel, I'm all set."\par \par \uc2 As a former member of the \uc2 U.S. Marine Corps\uc2 , Mr. Overlock is used to a pared-down look.\par \par \uc2 "Clothing is more of a necessity and a need in my world," he said. "When I think of shoes, I think of steel-toed boots."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220826ei8q0001u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
