\par\par \uc2 The Property Report\par \b \uc2 Lyft\uc2  to Sublease Space in Shift to Remote\b0\par\par\uc2 By Preetika Rana \par \uc2 513 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lyft Inc\uc2 . said it will sublease a significant part of some of its biggest U.S. offices, becoming the latest company to shrink its real-estate footprint to adjust to more employees working from home.\par \par \uc2 The ride-hailing company said Tuesday that it would rent out parts of its office space in San Francisco, New York City, Nashville and Seattle. Some 45% of the combined 615,000 square feet across those four locations will be leased to other businesses.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  plans to rent entire floors to others while maintaining separate entries for its own staff and operations.\par \par \uc2 In March, the company said its more than 4,000 office employees could work remotely indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 The drivers who use \uc2 Lyft\uc2  to match with riders aren't considered employees of the company.\par \par \uc2 "Many of our team members opted to work remotely after we shifted to a flexible workplace strategy," Rachel Goldstein, the \uc2 Lyft\uc2  executive managing the plan, said in a statement. "As a result, we have identified a significant amount of office space that isn't being utilized the way it previously was."\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's plans were earlier reported by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The move shows how companies are navigating a return to the office as threats from the pandemic recede.\par \par \uc2 Some companies are subleasing and vacating offices, hoping to cut costs as many employees continue to work from home and concerns about a potential economic downturn rise.\par \par \uc2 Software company \uc2 Salesforce Inc\uc2 . and business listing company \uc2 Yelp Inc\uc2 . are among the companies that have said they plan to sell or rent out parts of their office space.\par \par \uc2 Some companies are adding space even as they offer more flexibility to employees.\par \par \uc2 In January, \uc2 Google\uc2  said it planned to spend $1 billion buying real estate in central London. Last year, the company said it would spend $7 billion on more space for its offices and data centers in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Occupancy rates remain below prepandemic levels. In late June, the average occupancy rate in 10 major U.S. metro areas was less than 45%, down from over 95% before the pandemic began, according to \uc2 Kastle Systems\uc2 , which operates security systems in U.S. office buildings. \uc2 Kastle\uc2  tracks how many people are entering buildings based on anonymized data from its swipe-entry systems.\par \par \uc2 About 52% of companies expect to shrink their office space over the next three years, up from 44% a year earlier, a recent \uc2 CBRE\uc2  survey of 185 businesses with U.S. offices found. That is compared with 39% that intend to expand, up from 29% a year earlier, and 9% that foresee no change, down from 27% a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and its rival \uc2 Uber Technologies Inc\uc2 . have been looking for ways to trim costs as they try to pivot to profitable growth during a challenging macroeconomic environment.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  didn't disclose the financial implications of its plans to sublease its office space.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \uc2 Lyft\uc2  laid off about 60 employees and folded its car-rental business for riders. In May, \uc2 Uber\uc2  paused hiring.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220824ei8o0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
