\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Ohio Capital's Teachers Vote to Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Isabelle Sarraf \par \uc2 539 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ohio's largest public school district is scheduled to start classes online on Wednesday after teachers decided to strike over class sizes and other working conditions.\par \par \uc2 Teachers in Columbus are pushing primarily for smaller classes and functional heating and air-conditioning for their classrooms, said the Columbus Education Association union, which represents nearly 4,500 teachers and staff at Columbus City Schools.\par \par \uc2 Union members are also demanding full-time art, music and physical education teachers at the elementary level, as well as a cap on the number of class periods during a given school day.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for Columbus City Schools referred The Wall Street Journal to a recording of a press briefing given Monday by Board of Education President Jennifer Adair, and to the district's website. The organization says on its site: "The Board of Education is committed to providing an education to all of our students and is prepared to do that even during a teachers' strike."\par \par \uc2 The strike comes at a difficult time for schools nationwide, many of which are struggling to fill classroom vacancies and recruit qualified teachers before the year begins. In recent years, many teachers have retired early or left the profession altogether because of low pay and pandemic-related burnout.\par \par \uc2 A union representing about 2,000 Philadelphia staff, including bus drivers and custodial staff, also voted on Saturday to authorize a strike, demanding higher wages and training programs designed to help de-escalate potentially violent situations, like active shooters.\par \par \uc2 The earliest a strike could occur in Philadelphia is Sept. 1, three days after pre-K through Grade 12 students return to school. The School District of Philadelphia plans to participate in contract negotiations with the union, Superintendent Tony Watlington said.\par \par \uc2 The Columbus union's July negotiations with its State Employment Relations Board failed, but the two began hashing out a new collective bargaining agreement again on Aug. 10 at the request of a federal mediator.\par \par \uc2 The union in a statement said the board failed to respond to a counterproposal and didn't offer any proposals during this month's talks, leading its members to initiate a strike. The previous master agreement between the union and school district expired Sunday at midnight.\par \par \uc2 The school board said its final offer proposed installing air conditioning in nearly every school and reducing class sizes for kindergarten through the fifth grade to a maximum of 27 students.\par \par \uc2 The offer also included a guaranteed 3% annual salary increase for three years, a $2,000 bonus paid out over four payments and a new paid leave plan for parents, according to the district. By the end of the contract, a teacher who in the previous school year earned the district's average salary of $74,000 would earn upward of $91,000, according to the district. Teachers just starting out would earn more than $50,000 in their first year under the board's proposal.\par \par \uc2 Remote classes for Columbus students will tentatively be instructed by full-time substitutes, administrators and teachers who choose not to strike, according to the school district's back-to-school webpage, which features frequently asked questions pertaining to the strike.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220823ei8n0000u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
