\par\par \b \uc2 TikTok Curtails Perks for Employees\b0\par\par\uc2 By Salvador Rodriguez \par \uc2 680 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TikTok is getting rid of pandemic-era perks including daily meal stipends for many employees, according to people familiar with the situation, as the company pushes staff to work more from its offices.\par \par \uc2 TikTok announced its return to office policy July 11, requiring that employees come in at least two times a week but encouraging them to do so three times a week.\par \par \uc2 The company gave employees a little more than a month's notice that they would be expected back in the office, said the people familiar with the situation. The Chinese-owned social-media company hired thousands of employees after exploding in popularity following Covid-19 lockdowns in early 2020, with most of them working remotely.\par \par \uc2 Many of those people weren't working in places that corresponded with the locations of the offices where they were expected to be based. Those employees were advised that it is now time to begin moving to the location of their offices, a TikTok spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 Some employees said that as part of the return to work effort, they were told they would no longer be receiving reimbursement for Wi-Fi and gym bills. The spokeswoman said there hasn't been a companywide change to those types of expenses.\par \par \uc2 For U.S. workers not tied to the company's main hubs in Los Angeles and Mountain View, Calif., those employees are also losing a meal stipend of $45 a day, the people said. The company is working to bring food catering to its offices to make up for the loss of the meal stipend, the spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 "Our goal is to create a positive office environment for in-person collaboration that meets the needs of a global team while providing flexibility for employees to work remotely," the spokeswoman said. The company "will continue to offer various benefits to help make our hybrid work approach as smooth as possible."\par \par \uc2 TikTok's changes follow a string of moves and pronouncements from tech executives warning of belt-tightening designed to reduce costs and boost productivity.\par \par \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2 , the chief executive officer of \uc2 Google\uc2  parent \uc2 Alphabet Inc\uc2 . told staff last month to work with "greater urgency, sharper focus and more hunger than we've shown on sunnier days." \uc2 Meta Platforms Inc\uc2 . CEO \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  said in late July that the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  owner must operate with greater intensity. "I expect us to get more done with fewer resources," he said.\par \par \uc2 Some tech companies have also been scaling back the perks that have long been associated with the industry.\par \par \uc2 At some TikTok locations, the return of large numbers of employees has put a stress on some of the company's offices because there are not enough work stations, meeting rooms and parking spaces to accommodate everyone, the people familiar with the situation said.\par \par \uc2 The TikTok spokeswoman said the company has instituted desk-booking software that employees can use to ensure they will have a desk when they come in. The demand for desks hasn't exceeded supply on any day for any of the company's locations, she said, adding that TikTok is actively seeking to expand real estate to accommodate its workforce at several locations better.\par \par \uc2 In Mountain View, there are more than 1,200 employees, while the facility was only intended for 600 people, one of the people said. The Austin, Texas, office, which is two floors at a \uc2 WeWork\uc2  facility, now holds more than 600 employees. Some Austin employees have complained about the price of parking, which can be as high as $50 a day. The spokeswoman said the company is offering a $150 a month parking stipend to employees at some of its locations.\par \par \uc2 TikTok has allowed employees to apply for 60-day deferrals related to the return to office policy.\par \par \uc2 Employees can also apply for long-term remote work, which requires several levels of approval from human resources and the head of their department. Some approvals are going to Lidong Zhang, the chairman of the Chinese parent company, \uc2 ByteDance Ltd\uc2 ., one of the people said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220805ei8500011\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
