\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Big Labor Takes the House\b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 July 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Franklin Delano Roosevelt opposed unions for government workers because the employer and workers would essentially be on the same side of the bargaining table. Politicians would have an incentive to give workers what they want, and taxpayers would have no one representing their interests in negotiations. So it goes in states like Illinois and New York, where public unions wield extraordinary power, and that model may soon be coming to the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 Aides to eight progressive House members have filed petitions with the Office of Congressional Workplace Rights to begin the process of unionizing. This follows a House resolution in May to let staff collectively bargain for the first time in history. Staffers have complained for years about discrimination, pay and working conditions, and Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's Democrats viewed the resolution as part of their pro-union agenda.\par \par \uc2 Much as FDR predicted, look who's cheering the staff petitions -- their bosses on Capitol Hill. "I am so proud of the staffers who made a historic move today in seeking union recognition," said Michigan Rep. \uc2 Andy Levin\uc2 , who sponsored the May resolution. Missouri Rep. \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  said, "I am inspired by my staff and all those organizing who are leading by example." Minnesota's \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  enthused, "Every worker deserves a living wage and a union, including in the halls of Congress."\par \par \uc2 But wait, these Representatives won't be paying the salaries of their staff. Taxpayers will do that. And who is going to stand up for taxpayers when the union demands higher pay, overtime pay for working more than 35 hours a week, or protections against dismissal?\par \par \uc2 For now at least, each office must hold a separate unionizing vote, and so far few offices are taking that step. But that could change if a union gains a House foothold. Americans deserve to know their Representatives work for them, and they need a staff that answers to them and not also to a labor union. Republicans would be wise to repeal the union resolution if they retake the House in November.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: The Union and the Murderer" -- WSJ Aug. 9, 2022)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220730ei7u0001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
