\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Chicken Companies Settle Antitrust Case --- Wayne-Sanderson, Cargill agree to pay $85 million over wage-sharing claims\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick Thomas and Dave Michaels \par \uc2 699 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Cargill Inc\uc2 . and the newly formed \uc2 Wayne-Sanderson Farms\uc2  have agreed to pay $85 million to settle claims they violated antitrust laws by sharing information about poultry workers' wages.\par \par \uc2 The civil settlement between the companies and the government follows a lengthy review of the $4.5 billion sale of \uc2 Sanderson Farms Inc\uc2 . -- the third-largest U.S. poultry company -- to Cargill and agriculture investment firm \uc2 Continental Grain Co\uc2 . The settlement, which also addresses how chicken farmers are paid, came three days after the deal closed. The deal, which was first announced in August 2021, merged Sanderson's chicken operations with those of \uc2 Wayne Farms LLC\uc2 , a smaller chicken company owned by \uc2 Continental\uc2 , to form a new privately held company based in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department on Monday alleged that Cargill, \uc2 Sanderson Farms\uc2 , a data consulting firm and \uc2 Wayne Farms\uc2  for decades shared information about wages and benefits in a way that held down the pay for processing plant workers.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department also alleges that the chicken companies failed to give farmers enough information about the systems used to compensate them for raising birds for slaughter.\par \par \uc2 As part of Monday's settlement with the government, farmers who would raise chickens for Wayne-Sanderson would no longer be paid through the tournament system, a payment structure in which chicken companies measure farmers' performance against one another to determine how much they make. Farmers who perform well would still be offered bonuses.\par \par \uc2 The combined \uc2 Wayne-Sanderson Farms\uc2  represents roughly 15% of U.S. chicken production. \uc2 Tyson Foods Inc\uc2 . is the largest supplier of chicken in the U.S. with about one-fifth of the market, followed by \uc2 Pilgrim's Pride Corp\uc2 ., with about 17%, according to data from meat-industry publication Watt Poultry USA.\par \par \uc2 A Cargill spokesman said the merits of the deal outweigh the potential costs of any prolonged litigation. He said that the Justice Department's allegations lacked merit and that the company doesn't admit any wrongdoing. "Though we are disappointed with the assertions put forth by the Department of Justice and their approach to the review of our recent transaction, we have been cooperative throughout the process," the spokesman said. "We are pleased to have the review behind us."\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Wayne-Sanderson said the company is pleased to have the matter resolved.\par \par \uc2 As part of the settlement with the government, the companies will pay about $85 million in restitution to plant workers, of which Cargill will pay $15 million. The government would also impose a court-appointed compliance monitor to oversee processing facilities, farms and antitrust compliance for a decade, which the companies would have to fund.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department's complaint against the companies alleges they collaborated on compensation decisions by sharing information about wages directly and later through the data consultant's survey. The companies met annually in person, stressing their gatherings were confidential, to swap data on annual plant worker pay at sites around the country, the Justice Department's complaint said.\par \par \uc2 The companies engaged in "a brazen scheme to exchange wage and benefit information" that "harmed a generation of plant workers," said Doha Mekki, the Justice Department's second-ranking antitrust official.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration, lawmakers and regulators have ramped up antitrust scrutiny of the U.S. meat and poultry industries over the past year, in which a handful of big companies supply the bulk of the beef, chicken and pork eaten by U.S. consumers. The White House has frequently accused the biggest U.S. meat companies of using their market power to increase prices for restaurants and supermarkets while underpaying farmers and plant workers.\par \par \uc2 Monday's settlement with the government preventing \uc2 Wayne-Sanderson Farms\uc2  from using the tournament system is a major shake-up in how chicken companies have done business with farmers for decades. A shift away from the tournament model by one of the largest chicken companies could give rivals a competitive advantage or prompt a broader shift away from the model in the industry, poultry industry officials and analysts have said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220726ei7q00010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
