\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  Is Hit By One-Day Walkout Over Contract Talks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeffrey A. Trachtenberg \par \uc2 376 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 July 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Staffers at \uc2 HarperCollins Publishers\uc2  staged a one-day strike Wednesday following the breakdown of contract negotiations between the publisher and a union that represents some 250 of its employees.\par \par \uc2 The prior contract, which covers staffers in areas such as editorial, sales, publicity and other departments, expired Dec. 31 but was extended while the two sides continued to negotiate a new agreement.\par \par \uc2 Olga Brudastova, president of Local 2110 of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , the union representing the workers, said that raising member salaries is the union's first objective. Union members earn $55,000 a year, on average, while the lowest-paid staffers are paid $45,000, she said.\par \par \uc2 "The salaries don't represent the contributions our members are making," she said. "By striking, we're sending a message. This is a step in the process to a fair contract."\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  said the publishing house doesn't comment on negotiations. "Our hope is to continue our discussions at the negotiating table," she said.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal, like \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2 , is owned by \uc2 News Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another objective of the \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  staffers is to add specific language to the contract regarding diversity and inclusion efforts. They also want a commitment from \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  that staffers who don't join the union would still have to make a financial contribution to help cover the costs associated with collective bargaining.\par \par \uc2 The strike started at about 8:30 a.m. outside \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2 's office in lower Manhattan. About 75 to 100 staffers marched and chanted in favor of a new contract. Some carried homemade signs such as "Diversity in Publishing Matters" and "Where the Wild Things Are Underpaid." Leaflets were handed out.\par \par \uc2 "I'm here because I believe publishing can do better in terms of providing a living wage," said Stephanie Guerdan, an associate editor. "I love my job but I don't make the kind of money that allows for a sustainable career." She said that in addition to those who are protesting in person, some employees who work remotely are also refusing to work.\par \par \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  reported record profit for the fiscal year ended June 30, 2021, on a 19% gain in revenue to about $2 billion.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220721ei7l0000r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
