\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 OSHA\uc2  Investigates Complaints From Retailer's Warehouse Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allison Prang \par \uc2 470 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Federal authorities are investigating \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . warehouses after getting reports of health and safety violations.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Labor Department's Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  got referrals from the U.S. attorney's office for the Southern District of New York concerning allegations of safety and health violations at several \uc2 Amazon\uc2  facilities, a Labor Department spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the Southern District said \uc2 OSHA\uc2  on Monday morning went into \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouses outside New York City, Chicago and Orlando to inspect for workplace safety issues, including the pace of work for warehouse employees.\par \par \uc2 He said the civil division is investigating \uc2 Amazon\uc2  for safety hazards and "possible fraudulent conduct designed to hide injuries from \uc2 OSHA\uc2  and others."\par \par \uc2 "We'll of course cooperate with \uc2 OSHA\uc2  in their investigation," an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokeswoman said. "We believe it will ultimately show that these concerns are unfounded."\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse employees have complained about the pace at which they are asked to sort packages. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has required workers to meet certain work quotas per hour or face disciplinary measures.\par \par \uc2 The company has said it sets expectations based on employee experience, and also factors in health and safety considerations.\par \par \uc2 Last year, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  made a push to prevent workplace injuries by launching a program to teach employees how to not get injured and boost their mental health. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has said it is aiming to cut recordable incidents by half come 2025.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2 , who took over for founder \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  in the summer of 2021, said in his first shareholder letter earlier this year that he wanted to improve worker safety.\par \par \uc2 In the letter, he said \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's injury rates were "sometimes misunderstood" and that warehouse incidents were slightly higher than peers' average and slightly under peers' average in the courier and delivery industry -- putting the company overall on par with others.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bezos, who currently serves as \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's executive chairman, has characterized reports of unsafe working conditions at the company's warehouses as inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the company has been facing efforts among workers to unionize. Earlier this year, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers at a Staten Island warehouse voted in favor of doing so. Another warehouse on the island a short while later voted against it.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  in April posted its first quarterly loss since 2015 because of inflation, supply-chain hiccups and a decline in online shopping.\par \par \uc2 The company's stake in \uc2 Rivian Automotive Inc\uc2 ., the electric-vehicle company, also affected results. Revenue climbed by about 7%, the company's slowest pace in roughly two decades.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  shares rose $4.45, or 3.9%, in Tuesday trading to finish at $118.21.\par \par \uc2 The stock is down about 33% over the past 12 months and down 29% year to date.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Sebastian Herrera contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220720ei7k00013\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
