\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: U.S. Will Intervene In Freight Railroad Labor Rift\b0\par\par\uc2 By Esther Fung \par \uc2 309 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 July 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Biden appointed a federal panel that seeks to resolve a labor dispute between freight railroads and their labor unions, forestalling a possible strike that would disrupt already strained services.\par \par \uc2 Railroads including \uc2 BNSF Railway\uc2 , \uc2 CSX Transportation Inc\uc2 ., \uc2 Union Pacific Railroad Co\uc2 . and \uc2 Norfolk Southern Railway\uc2 , and 12 unions, have been in contract talks since late 2019, after the existing contract came open for renewal.\par \par \uc2 The White House faced a Sunday deadline to intervene, as requested by retailers and other shippers worried about freight service. Members of one union representing more than 23,000 train-service employees at major railroads voted recently to authorize a strike.\par \par \uc2 The three-member panel will investigate the disputes and try to broker a settlement on wage raises and benefits. The recommendations of the Presidential Emergency Board, which are nonbinding, give railroads and unions more time to work out a new contract for around 115,000 workers.\par \par \uc2 "These disputes threaten substantially to interrupt interstate commerce to a degree that would deprive a section of the country of essential transportation service," Mr. Biden said, according to the executive order issued by the White House on Friday.\par \par \uc2 These emergency boards have been called to settle railroad labor disputes periodically since the 1930s. The appointment of the panel staves off a strike for an additional 60 days.\par \par \uc2 Railroad workers said they have gone without pay raises for a few years as negotiations dragged on. The Covid-19 pandemic, rising inflation and labor shortages have made working and living conditions more challenging, rail workers said.\par \par \uc2 Railroad companies are also contending with growing stacks of containers in freight hubs, pointing out that labor and equipment shortages are causing the bottlenecks. The congestion delays the time it takes to get goods to distribution centers and stores.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220716ei7g0002w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
