\par\par \b \uc2 Welcome to the Full-Employment Recession\b0\par\par\uc2 By James Piereson \par \uc2 1,140 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 July 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Is the U.S. economy in a recession? Real gross domestic product contracted at an annual rate of 1.6% in the first quarter of 2022, according to a recent estimate by the \uc2 Bureau of Economic Analysis\uc2 . The \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta\uc2  estimates that the economy contracted by a seasonally adjusted 1.2% in the second quarter. If those estimates turn out to be correct, the U.S. economy will have contracted for two consecutive quarters, the technical definition of a recession.\par \par \uc2 This recession -- if that's what it is -- isn't like other recessions. According to the latest employment report issued by the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , the economy added 372,000 new jobs in June, with the unemployment rate remaining stable at 3.6%. Over the past 12 months, according to the same report, average hourly earnings increased by 5.1%, another sign of a tight labor market. What explains a full-employment recession? The answer lies in long-term developments in the U.S. labor force.\par \par \uc2 The economy grew by 5.7% in 2021, the largest annual gain since the 1980s, with an addition of some 6.1 million new jobs. But those gains were in the form of bounce-backs from unprecedented losses in 2020 because of the coronavirus pandemic. The economy contracted by 3.4% in 2020 and some nine million jobs disappeared. When the GDP changes from 2020 and 2021 are combined and averaged, the annual growth rate over these two years comes in at just above 2%, very close to the 2.3% rate in 2019, before the Covid lockdowns began.\par \par \uc2 Economic growth has been declining decade by decade since the 1960s, when annual real GDP advanced by an average of 4.5% before falling to 3.3% in the 1970s, 3.1% in the 1980s and 3.2% in the 1990s. Economic growth then slowed to 1.9% a year from 2000 to 2009, thanks in part to the deep recession and financial crisis of 2008, and to 2.2% from 2010 to 2019 as the economy gradually recovered from that crisis. A growth rate of around 2% a year, or less than half the rate of the 1960s, appears to be the "new normal" for the U.S. economy. Despite the robust labor market, the country remains stuck in a long cycle of slowing growth.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  has reported monthly since 1948 on the size of the civilian labor force in the U.S. This is a comprehensive measure of the labor force that includes all people over 16 who are either employed or seeking employment. The civilian labor force grew at uneven rates from 61.6 million in 1950 to 163 million in 2021, but growth has slowed substantially since the late 1990s.\par \par \uc2 During the 1950s, the workforce expanded at an average rate of 1.1% a year, with between 600,000 and a million workers added annually. It then accelerated during the 1960s as the workforce expanded at an average rate of 1.7% a year, with an addition of some 13 million new workers, a nearly 20% increase during the decade. The annual expansion of the workforce peaked during the 1970s with an average gain of 2.7% a year as the baby boom generation entered the workforce, with substantial increases in female workers adding to the overall totals. During that decade the labor force expanded by more than 24 million workers, or nearly 30%.\par \par \uc2 The labor force expanded at slower rates during the next two decades, by an annual average of 1.8% in the 1980s and 1.3% in the 1990s, owing to lower birthrates from the late 1960s through the 1970s. All told, the workforce grew by 18 million from 1980 to 1989 and by nearly 16 million from 1990 to 1999. At that point, expansion of the labor force fell sharply, to an average of 0.9% a year from 2000 to 2009, or by 13 million new workers during the decade, and 0.6% a year from 2010 to 2019, or 11.3 million new workers. Over the past two decades the workforce expanded by less than half the rate of the 1980s and by less than a third the rate of the 1970s, an important cause of slowing economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The labor-force participation rate has also been declining in recent decades, thereby adding to the challenge of slowing population growth. The percentage of the adult population working or actively looking for work increased steadily from 59% in 1965 to 67% in the late 1990s, followed by a steady drop-off to 62% in 2022. With the current labor force at around 163 million workers, a 4% decline represents about six million jobs.\par \par \uc2 Part of this decline reflects the aging of the population: The baby boomers are retiring. In addition, the labor-force participation rate among men has fallen from 88% in 1950 to 70% today, which mostly accounts for the overall drop in the participation rate. As men represent about 54% of the labor force, a decline of 18% is the equivalent of nine million or 10 million jobs in 2022.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, labor force participation for women increased from 35% in the 1950s to 60% by the late 1990s, neutralizing the fall-off among men. The increased participation among women in that era undoubtedly boosted the growth rate from the 1960s through the 1990s. Female participation has declined slightly in recent years, to 58%, so that this element in American economic growth appears to have run its course.\par \par \uc2 These two factors account for the long-term slowdown in economic growth juxtaposed against a low unemployment rate. The growth of the labor force has gradually slowed over the past few decades, with obvious effects on overall economic growth. Today's low unemployment rate obscures the substantial decline in workforce participation in recent years, as "dropouts" from the workforce aren't included in the unemployment figures. If they were added, as some analysts think they should be, then the real unemployment rate wouldn't be 3.6% but more like 9% or 10%.\par \par \uc2 There are no obvious solutions to this problem. Rising wages should draw more workers into the labor market. A rational immigration policy might bring skilled workers from abroad into the labor force. New information technologies may improve worker productivity, but no such payoffs are yet visible in those statistics. Lacking real solutions, Americans may have to adjust to a future of slowing economic growth, with all that implies for intergenerational progress, American strength abroad and political conflict at home.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Piereson is a senior fellow at the Manhattan Institute.\par \par \uc2 (See related letters: "Letters to the Editor: Recession, Growth and Debt" -- WSJ July 22, 2022)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220715ei7f0000y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
