\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Workers Make Big Push for Unions, U.S. Data Show\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Harrison and Heather Haddon \par \uc2 676 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The number of U.S. workplaces where employees have started trying to organize unions jumped this year to the highest level in half a dozen years, a rise that reflects warming public attitudes toward unions amid a strong labor market.\par \par \uc2 In the first half of the year, workers at 1,411 U.S. workplaces filed petitions with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , the first step in joining a union, according to a Wall Street Journal analysis of federal data. That represents a 69% increase from the same period in 2021.\par \par \uc2 The union push comes as public opinion about organized labor is the most positive in decades. A Gallup poll last year found that 68% of Americans approve of unions, the highest share since 1965.\par \par \uc2 The workers filing petitions this year include some at \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 ., \uc2 Starbucks Corp\uc2 ., and \uc2 Chipotle Mexican Grill Inc\uc2 ., as well as grocery stores, hospitals and major retailers. Many employees say they are seeking better health and safety precautions, more pay and more say over their working conditions.\par \par \uc2 For small-business owners, the increase in unionization is adding another expense on top of rising costs, a labor shortage and ongoing supply-chain disruptions, said Beth Milito, senior executive counsel at the \uc2 National Federation of Independent Business\uc2 . "It's really challenging right now for business owners and then if you layer on any other sort of employee-management issue that could be related to a union organizing drive it gets more complex and more expensive," she said.\par \par \uc2 Brandi McNease, a crew member at a Chipotle restaurant in Augusta, Maine, said employees there were frustrated about low staffing levels that required people to work back-to-back shifts with little support or guidance from management. In June, workers staged a walkout, then filed a petition with the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  to form an independent union. "We all just decided it was time to start treating restaurant workers like people," Ms. McNease said.\par \par \uc2 Laurie Schalow\uc2 , Chipotle's chief corporate affairs officer, said the company immediately closed the restaurant when it learned about the employees' concerns so it could hire new staff and bring in new leadership. "We respect our employees' rights under the National Labor Relations Act and are committed to ensuring a fair and just work environment that provides opportunities for all," she said.\par \par \uc2 Of the 1,411 petitions filed with the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  in the first half of this year, 311 have come from \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores. Of those, 145 locations have voted to join a union and 22 have voted against, according to \uc2 NLRB\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 In December 2021, a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in Buffalo, N.Y., became the first U.S. company-owned location to form a union during the current push after workers there sought better pay and working conditions.\par \par \uc2 "To be honest, I didn't know what I was signing up for," said Kylah Clay, a barista from Boston whose Allston, Mass., location was one of the first \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to file to unionize after the Buffalo stores. "I never imagined it would turn into something so national."\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokesman said the company is focused on working with its employees directly but respects their right to organize. The company will bargain in good faith, he said.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , health concerns at a warehouse on Staten Island in New York prompted workers to file an \uc2 NLRB\uc2  petition. In April, workers there voted in favor of forming a union, the company's first U.S. facility to unionize.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has offered Covid-19 testing and vaccines to employees as well as paid time off and bonuses for workers to get vaccinated, said spokeswoman Maria Boschetti.\par \par \uc2 "Our employees have the choice of whether or not to join a union. They always have," said \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokeswoman Kelly Nantel. "As a company, we don't think unions are the best answer for our employees. Our focus remains on working directly with our team to continue making \uc2 Amazon\uc2  a great place to work."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich494\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7410
