\par\par \b \uc2 Strike Talks Stall at Machinery Maker CNH\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Tita \par \uc2 337 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Negotiations between \uc2 CNH Industrial NV\uc2  and the equipment maker's striking workers have hit a stalemate, deepening supply-chain problems with farm and construction equipment.\par \par \uc2 Bargaining sessions between the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  and London-based \uc2 CNH\uc2  broke down in the middle of June, and there are no additional meetings scheduled in the near term, union leaders and the company said.\par \par \uc2 CNH\uc2 , whose brands include \uc2 Case IH\uc2  and New Holland, is the second-largest farm equipment manufacturer in the U.S. behind \uc2 Deere & Co\uc2 ., based on sales. About 1,100 hourly workers in Burlington, Iowa, and Racine, Wis., have been on strike since May 2 at the company, which in 2021 sold about $18 billion worth of tractors, excavators and other farm and construction equipment world-wide.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  wants higher wages and a longer contract than the company has offered. The union said the wage increases proposed by the company would be eaten up by the higher costs the company wants employees to shoulder for health insurance.\par \par \uc2 "This deal has to sell itself and right now it's not selling itself," said Nick Guernsey, president of the union local in Burlington, where \uc2 CNH\uc2  produces Case-brand construction equipment, including backhoes, and components.\par \par \uc2 CNH\uc2  said \uc2 UAW\uc2  negotiators rejected the company's last major proposal in May without putting it before rank-and-file members. "We strongly encourage the \uc2 UAW\uc2  to present our offer to our employees for a vote," the company said.\par \par \uc2 CNH\uc2 , like other equipment manufacturers, has struggled to obtain semiconductors and other key components in short supply during the past 18 months. New equipment fresh off assembly lines has languished at \uc2 CNH\uc2 's plants waiting for parts to complete them. Some dealers said the strike has further lengthened wait times for new machinery and components.\par \par \uc2 "The strike has made it worse," said Rod Bierle, manager at Bancroft Implement, a \uc2 Case IH\uc2  dealer in Iowa. "They need to settle the strike. We're waiting for tractors."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220711ei7b0000v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
