\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Heard on the Street: The Fed Can't Print More Workers --- Rate hikes could overcool parts of the labor market\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 675 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is raising rates, the U.S. economy is weakening and recession talk is on the rise. But the biggest impediment to the job market still isn't any of those things -- it is the availability of workers.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department on Friday said the economy added a seasonally adjusted 372,000 jobs in June, well above the 250,000 economists expected. Meanwhile, the unemployment rate was unchanged at 3.6%, putting it near a 50-year low.\par \par \uc2 Despite the gains in employment, there are still 524,000 fewer jobs than there were in February 2020, before the Covid-19 crisis hit. Allowing for population growth, there would need to be something on the order of three million jobs to declare a complete recovery for employment.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the low unemployment rate owes a lot to people exiting the labor force. Only those actively seeking work count as unemployed. If labor-force participation -- the share of the working-age population employed or seeking employment -- were as high now as it was before the pandemic, the unemployment rate would be 5.5%.\par \par \uc2 If more people were to enter the workforce then it is likely that many of them could quickly find jobs. As much as businesses have been hiring, a lot would like to hire even more. Labor Department figures released show that, as of the end of May, there were 11.3 million job openings compared with seven million prior to the Covid crisis.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, with an aging population there is reason to think labor-force participation would be somewhat lower than in February 2020, pandemic or no pandemic. And in some cases the pandemic probably led people to rethink their priorities, taking them out of the workforce. But that isn't the whole story.\par \par \uc2 Recently released data from the Census Bureau, based on a June survey that partly overlapped with the period when the Labor Department was collecting its employment figures, showed that an estimated 3.7 million weren't working because they were ill with Covid symptoms or caring for someone who was. An additional 2.7 million weren't working because they were concerned about getting or spreading Covid. And a further 2.9 million weren't working because they were laid off or furloughed because of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, there were 2.1 million who weren't working because their employer closed temporarily or went out of business due to the pandemic. Also, 5.8 million weren't working because they were caring for children not in school or daycare -- a number that would likely be lower if it weren't for the difficulties working parents have had obtaining child care since the pandemic started. (There was no doubling up of reasons for not working; survey participants were allowed only one response.)\par \par \uc2 So it seems likely that if Covid cases dissipate, allowing for a fuller reopening of the economy, a lot more people could be employed or seeking employment. They might not ever get their chance, though, if the Fed raises rates to the point where the job market buckles. One danger is that, if that happens, the skills of many of the people who are now out of work might erode to the point where they have a harder time finding employment.\par \par \uc2 As a result, what might now be a temporary reduction in labor-force participation could become permanent. The result would be an economy that couldn't grow as quickly without generating inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Fed is trying to get the inflation that is occurring now under control, of course. But, with a variety of signs suggesting the prices are starting to moderate, the next big debate could be what it should do about a job market that in some respects seems too hot and in others doesn't seem so hot at all.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich474\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7110
