\par\par \b \uc2 Pilot Strike Tips SAS Into Chapter 11 Filing\b0\par\par\uc2 By Benjamin Katz and Sara Ruberg \par \uc2 1,006 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Scandinavian airline \uc2 SAS AB\uc2  filed for bankruptcy protection in the U.S., saying a move by about 1,000 of its pilots to go on strike would worsen its already fraught finances as the carrier becomes one of the first casualties of a difficult recovery in air travel.\par \par \uc2 The company said Tuesday that it would continue to operate during the voluntary restructuring process, but the staff walkout would lead it to cancel about 50% of its flights daily. SAS said it expects the chapter 11 court process to last between nine and 12 months.\par \par \uc2 SAS has been pursuing a restructuring plan for months to shore up its finances after racking up large amounts of debt and experiencing a sharp drop in revenue during the Covid-19 pandemic. The airline said its decision to file for bankruptcy protection was designed to accelerate that transformation. Filing in the U.S. was favorable because it gave management more flexibility to negotiate with stakeholders while continuing to operate, it said.\par \par \uc2 While SAS is only Western Europe's 14th-largest airline by capacity, according to aviation consulting firm OAG, the carrier is critical to Scandinavia's connectivity, operating the highest capacity in and out of the region.\par \par \uc2 The broader aviation industry is struggling to increase capacity to meet surging demand for air travel after more than two years of pandemic lockdowns and travel restrictions. Staff shortages and a tight labor market have led to long lines, mounds of lost luggage, delays and canceled flights while many unions have been pushing to recover salary cuts agreed to at the outset of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 While scores of airlines have moved through bankruptcy proceedings since March 2020, fewer carriers failed than the industry had expected as government bailouts and furlough programs helped keep most afloat during the hiatus in flying. However, many of those airlines were left saddled with billions of dollars of repayable state aid.\par \par \uc2 This summer's recovery had been foreshadowed as a critical resurgence in air travel that would help airlines with heavily indebted balance sheets to recover revenue and return to profitability. However, the at-times chaotic nature of the recovery has curtailed some of those hopes.\par \par \uc2 While airlines in the U.S. have reined in capacity this summer because of airport constraints, staffing shortfalls and weather disruptions, including cutting 15% of what they had planned to fly this summer at the outset of the year, operators in Europe have been hit more acutely.\par \par \uc2 Travel restrictions in Europe had meant flights between countries recovered later compared with the relatively quick rebound in domestic traffic in the U.S. that helped bolster U.S. carriers and keep airports staffed at relatively higher levels.\par \par \uc2 The rapid recovery across Europe has put the region's aviation industry under significant strain. Airlines including \uc2 Deutsche Lufthansa AG\uc2 , \uc2 Air France-KLM\uc2  and discount operator \uc2 easyJet PLC\uc2  have each cut thousands of flights from their schedules, many at short notice, to limit congestion and deal with mounting delays. Meanwhile, airports including hubs such as London Gatwick Airport and Amsterdam Schiphol have restricted the number of flights they can handle to reduce passenger traffic through their terminals.\par \par \uc2 British Airways\uc2  became the latest on Tuesday to announce new cuts, saying it would cancel more flights this summer than initially planned to ease congestion across its network. The airline now plans to scrap 11% of its total capacity, mostly on short-haul flights in Europe, from earlier plans to cut 10% through October, amounting to the additional cancellation of more than 1,000 flights. The decision follows the \uc2 U.K. government\uc2 's move to offer airlines a reprieve on takeoff-slot rules that require carriers to operate a minimum number of flights to retain their departure times at airports. \uc2 BA\uc2  is also facing a walkout from check-in staff over the summer.\par \par \uc2 SAS entered the pandemic with what executives described as an uncompetitive cost structure. The crisis dented cash reserves and added debt.\par \par \uc2 With its restructuring, the company is seeking to slash costs, adjust its fleet and rework its network.\par \par \uc2 However, pilots at SAS, which has hubs in Denmark, Sweden and Norway, announced plans on Monday to go on strike after talks with management broke down. The airline has been negotiating wage cuts with its crews to support its restructuring, but pilots have complained that the company has hired new pilots on cheaper contracts through two new subsidiaries instead of bringing back staffers who were let go over the past two years.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, SAS said the strike had led it to accelerate the filing for bankruptcy protection. The airline had warned the previous day that the walkout could jeopardize its future.\par \par \uc2 "We've been working hard to improve our cost structure and improve our financial position," SAS Chief Executive \uc2 Anko van der Werff\uc2  said. "We are making progress, but a lot of work remains and the ongoing strike has made an already challenging situation even tougher."\par \par \uc2 The company, in which Denmark and Sweden each own a 21.8% stake, said the bankruptcy filing is aimed at restructuring SAS's debt obligations and helping management negotiate new agreements with key stakeholders. Those include bondholders, leasing companies and aircraft manufacturers. The carrier currently has outstanding orders with \uc2 Airbus SE\uc2  for two A350 wide-body jets and 30 A320neo narrow-body jets, according to the plane maker's available data. An \uc2 Airbus\uc2  spokesman declined to comment on the status of a customer's business.\par \par \uc2 Discussions with potential lenders to obtain as much as $700 million in emergency funding to support operations through the court process are "well-advanced," the company said.\par \par \uc2 Under SAS's restructuring plan, made public in February, the airline is aiming to reduce costs by about $720 million, raise around $910 million in fresh equity and reduce or convert into equity more than $1.9 billion of debt. SAS filed for bankruptcy in the U.S. Bankruptcy Court in the Southern District of New York.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220706ei760001u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
