\par\par \b \uc2 Demand For Chips Eases as Sales of PCs Slow\b0\par\par\uc2 By Asa Fitch \par \uc2 822 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 July 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The pandemic-era boom in semiconductors that spurred a global shortage is showing its first signs of weakness, driven by a slump in personal-computer sales and a rout in cryptocurrency markets.\par \par \uc2 The frenzy to buy laptops and other gadgets early in the Covid-19 pandemic has vanished as inflation dissuades people from upgrading machines that they bought in the past couple of years amid the shift toward remote work and learning. The fading of the crypto boom also put an end to early pandemic scenes of people camping outside computer stores to buy chips for cryptocurrency mining and high-end videogaming.\par \par \uc2 The pressure isn't easing in some in-demand areas, such as chips for cars and data centers, but the chillier consumer climate prompted some giants such as \uc2 Intel Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Nvidia Corp\uc2 . to warn of rockier months ahead after two years of surging demand across their product lineups.\par \par \uc2 David Zinsner\uc2 , \uc2 Intel\uc2 's chief financial officer, said in June that the outlook for the second half of this year had gotten "a lot noisier" over the previous month, and that the company would look to align spending and investments to that reality. \uc2 Intel\uc2  temporarily froze hiring in its PC-chip division in June, among other belt-tightening measures.\par \par \uc2 Memory maker \uc2 Micron Technologies Inc\uc2 . last week issued a muted sales outlook as Chief Executive \uc2 Sanjay Mehrotra\uc2  warned that "the industry demand environment has weakened," with PC and smartphone sales declining. The company said it was cutting back on some spending plans to adjust to the new market dynamics.\par \par \uc2 Chip executives, computer retailers and distributors say the market malaise has gotten worse in recent months -- more sluggish than many were expecting at the beginning of the year as U.S. inflation hit 8.6% in May, the highest in more than four decades.\par \par \uc2 "The up went way up, and the down went way down," said Kent Tibbils, vice president of marketing at \uc2 ASI Corp\uc2 ., a major electronics distributor based in California, pointing to a falloff in demand for computers around March. "Usually there's more of a gradual line, but it's been a little bit more volatile than what we might normally expect."\par \par \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  said it is dialing back hiring as the chip maker braces for slowdowns in two of its key areas, crypto mining and videogames. The company's chips are used for the heavy-duty computational work required to mine new cryptocurrency tokens, and they are prized by videogamers who bought its graphics cards during a pandemic-era surge in home-based entertainment. Both areas are weakening, and \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 's stock fell 48% in the first half of the year.\par \par \uc2 Personal computer shipments are expected to retreat by 8.2% this year to 321.2 million units, according to \uc2 International Data Corp\uc2 ., which cut its forecast in June. Those numbers are a sharp reversal from the height of the pandemic, when shipments grew 13% in its first year and 15% in the second.\par \par \uc2 Advanced Micro Devices Inc\uc2 . Chief Executive \uc2 Lisa Su\uc2  said last month that the company already had taken a conservative view of the PC segment for this year and expects computer demand to be roughly flat over the next few years.\par \par \uc2 Computer-makers \uc2 HP Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Dell Technologies Inc\uc2 . said they are seeing principally lower-end consumer PC demand softening, slightly offset by healthy sales to companies.\par \par \uc2 PC shipments have been hit by not just softening appetite for new devices, but also supply-chain snarls from Covid lockdowns in China, where many computers are assembled, and the fallout from Western sanctions on Russia after the country's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Analysts have grown more pessimistic about chip companies' sales prospects. In February, analysts estimated \uc2 Intel\uc2  would generate sales of around $18.4 billion in the second quarter, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . Now the average estimate is for less than $18 billion, below what \uc2 Intel\uc2  has projected. Estimates for \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 's sales have also decreased 4% over the period, and are in line with the company's $8.1 billion sales guidance for the current quarter.\par \par \uc2 "Semi downturns happen every 3-4 years, and we could be due for another one," \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  analysts said last week.\par \par \uc2 It isn't just chip makers that are resetting their expectations. \uc2 Microsoft Corp\uc2 . lowered its earnings outlook in June, citing a stronger dollar that has dented the value of its sales in foreign countries, and the company has slowed hiring.\par \par \uc2 A potential silver lining for those customers who are still in shopping mode is that wait times for some semiconductors are starting to ease after swelling during the two-year-long chip drought. Chip lead times overall were at a near-record 27 weeks in May, according to Susquehanna Financial Group, but wait times for microcontrollers -- ubiquitous chips that have been a bugbear for auto makers and others -- shortened.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220705ei7500027\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
