\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Data Suggest Job-Market Slowdown\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Chaney Cambon \par \uc2 762 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 June 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly every labor indicator suggests the hottest job market in half a century is starting to cool as signs mount that U.S. economic growth is slowing.\par \par \uc2 Employers are still hiring and layoffs are low, but the labor market is likely past its peak as the broader economy slows and could even contract in the second quarter.\par \par \uc2 A labor-market slowdown means fewer opportunities for workers. At the same time, it is good news for \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials: The central bank is ratcheting up interest rates to bring down high inflation, and its officials say demand for workers also needs to come down to bring stability to prices.\par \par \uc2 How fast and steep could hiring cool off? Are we heading for a period of higher unemployment? Here are the signals that economists are watching to gauge labor-market momentum:\par \par \uc2 Labor demand\par \par \uc2 By March of this year, job openings hit a record 11.9 million. But the momentum slipped in April as openings declined to 11.4 million, a historically high level but a sign that employer demand for workers might be headed downward.\par \par \uc2 Other job-postings figures show that demand for workers, while still strong, is moderating. For instance, job-postings growth on \uc2 Indeed.com\uc2  compared with February 2020 has flatlined at an elevated level this year. Growth in software-development job postings on Indeed remains well above that for overall job postings but has cooled in recent weeks as some technology firms announced hiring freezes and layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  has said employer demand for workers is too strong. In his postmeeting news conference this week, he noted there are roughly two job vacancies for every person seeking work. Lowering the excess demand for workers could help bring wage growth down to a healthy level that is more consistent with 2% inflation, he said.\par \par \uc2 Layoffs\par \par \uc2 Jobless claims, a weekly proxy for layoffs, have steadily climbed in recent weeks after hitting a 53-year low this spring. The gradual rise in initial jobless claims could reflect the recent layoffs in industries such as technology, retail and real estate. Economists and investors are closely watching weekly claims because they tend to be a leading indicator of recessions.\par \par \uc2 Claims are far too low to indicate a recession is imminent. Their recent rise, however, adds to signals that the labor market is easing. Softer payroll growth in May offered another indication that red-hot demand for labor is beginning to cool, economists note.\par \par \uc2 Pay gains\par \par \uc2 Wage growth is showing signs of decelerating. Average hourly earnings grew 5.2% in May from a year earlier, down from a recent peak of 5.6% in March.\par \par \uc2 Workers at restaurants and bars were among the biggest recipients of pay increases last year as companies scrambled to hire. Those wage rises, though, appear to be slowing sharply. Leisure and hospitality pay was up 10.3% in May from a year earlier, a steep slowdown from a high of 13.3% in December.\par \par \uc2 The wage-growth pullback should help ease any concerns of a wage-price spiral, some economists say. A spiral occurs when workers demand higher wages to offset price increases, triggering companies to raise prices more to compensate.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment rate\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate has remained at 3.6% for three consecutive months, near the prepandemic half-century low of 3.5%. Fed officials recently started to signal that a higher unemployment rate might be a necessary consequence of their efforts to reduce inflation. Wednesday's economic projections showed officials expect the unemployment rate to end the year at 3.7% before rising to 4.1% in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Whether the jobless rate rises to only 4.1% is an open question. Today's tight labor market is a result of too few workers to meet strong consumer spending. If consumers slash their spending amid higher inflation and interest rates, unemployment might rise more than current projections suggest.\par \par \uc2 Quits\par \par \uc2 In a strong job market, workers are more likely to quit their jobs for better opportunities. Bright job-market prospects helped push the quits rate, or the share of workers leaving their jobs, to a record 3% at the end of last year. However, the rate has since ticked down, which matters for wage growth and inflation.\par \par \uc2 Workers who quit their jobs often command bigger raises. A slide in the quits rate could tug down wage gains and ultimately price increases. That is because employers, facing lower labor costs, might feel less financial pressure to pass along higher prices.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw440\pich640\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6600\pichgoal9600
