\par\par \b \uc2 Musk's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Chat: Staff, Growth, Aliens\b0\par\par\uc2 By Deepa Seetharaman and Sarah E. Needleman \par \uc2 949 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , speaking via a smartphone that he appeared to be holding in front of him, told \uc2 Twitter Inc\uc2 . employees that he wants to dramatically increase the platform's users and didn't rule out layoffs as he works to complete his $44 billion takeover.\par \par \uc2 According to employees who participated in the wide-ranging roughly 45-minute virtual meeting, Mr. Musk also outlined his thoughts on free speech and discussed the possibility of alien life.\par \par \uc2 The billionaire serial entrepreneur's appearance was another twist in what has been an unusual takeover bid.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk's first direct interaction with \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's rank-and-file employees comes after weeks in which he publicly criticized the company, belittled its top executives and threatened to walk away from the deal.\par \par \uc2 Since inking the acquisition in April, he has said \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is too reliant on advertising, questioned its estimate of how many of its users are bots and tweeted a poop emoji at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Parag Agrawal\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Agrawal introduced Mr. Musk at the meeting Thursday, according to the people who attended. Mr. Musk was then asked questions by \uc2 Leslie Berland\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's chief marketing officer, that employees had submitted.\par \par \uc2 Regarding layoffs, Mr. Musk said anyone who is a significant contributor shouldn't have anything to worry about, according to people who viewed the meeting. "Right now, costs exceed revenue," he said, according to the people. "That's not a great situation."\par \par \uc2 One employee tweeted, "still not sure if I need to start packing my bags."\par \par \uc2 Like other companies, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has taken steps recently to rein in costs, including issuing a hiring freeze, rescinding some job offers and asking employees to curtail business travel and spending. As part of the cuts, the company also canceled a companywide trip to Disneyland that was set for January 2023, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  confirmed.\par \par \uc2 When asked about what he would consider as successful for \uc2 Twitter\uc2  five to 10 years from now, Mr. Musk said a substantial increase in daily active users to over a billion from around 229 million today. Mr. Musk also said during the meeting that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  should be entertaining like TikTok and said he admired the Chinese app \uc2 WeChat\uc2 , which is used heavily in China for a broad range of purposes including e-commerce and social networking.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk said that he learns a lot from what he reads on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and that it is the best way to communicate with a lot of people simultaneously, according to people familiar with the meeting.\par \par \uc2 Asked about his stance on free speech, Mr. Musk drew a distinction between freedom of speech and freedom of reach, according to people who attended the session. Mr. Musk explained that that meant people should be allowed to say pretty outrageous things within the law but didn't necessarily deserve their tweets to be amplified and spread virally across \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On whether he would be CEO, Mr. Musk said he isn't hung up on titles. He went on to explain his unifying philosophy to extend the scope, scale and lifespan of civilization as we know it, according to a person who viewed the meeting.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk also briefly segued into a discussion about the prospects of alien life. "I've seen no actual evidence for aliens . . . yet," he told employees, people who attended the meeting said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Berland raised the subject of remote work, which Mr. Musk has criticized on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . About 1,500 of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's employees work remotely, Ms. Berland said in the meeting. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  previously announced a plan to allow employees to work remotely indefinitely if it helped them be more productive.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk responded that the work of building cars at electric-vehicle maker \uc2 Tesla Inc\uc2 ., where he is chief executive, can't be done remotely.\par \par \uc2 "If someone can only work remotely, and they're exceptional, it wouldn't make sense to fire them," Mr. Musk said, according to the people who viewed the meeting. "Definitely not in favor of things that are mad." He added that his bias is strongly for working in person but that managers can decide which of their employees can work remotely, as is the practice at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Soon after Mr. Musk's comments, some employees publicly tweeted jokes and memes about how to convey they were exceptional. One employee tweeted: "adding exceptional contributor to my CV."\par \par \uc2 In response to a question about how employees and Mr. Musk can earn each other's trust, Mr. Musk said: "Trust is as trust does. I tend to be extremely literal in what I say."\par \par \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is aiming to schedule a shareholder vote on the deal this summer and close it soon thereafter, barring unforeseen developments. Mr. Musk said he would come back for another question-and-answer session, the people said.\par \par \uc2 Manu Cornet, an engineer at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , poked fun at the all-hands meeting with a cartoon he tweeted Thursday, suggesting details of the session would be leaked.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk has kept \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employees and Wall Street on edge as to whether he will follow through on his agreed-upon purchase of the social-media platform.\par \par \uc2 Last week, he threatened to terminate the transaction in a letter accusing the company of not complying with his request for data. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  at the time said it was continuing to share information with Mr. Musk and that it planned to enforce the merger agreement.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk agreed to pay $54.20 a share for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , though the company's share price has since declined amid broader market declines. The stock closed at $37.36 Thursday.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Meghan Bobrowsky and Sarah Krouse contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220617ei6h00022\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
