\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 On the Clock: Want a Promotion? Try To Not Be So Remote\b0\par\par\uc2 By Callum Borchers \par \uc2 1,017 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 June 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employees of accounting firm \uc2 Dixon Hughes Goodman\uc2  showed CEO \uc2 Matt Snow\uc2  that they could be productive at home during the pandemic. So, last fall, the company declared hybrid the new normal and made the office optional on most days.\par \par \uc2 This month, the firm merged with a larger one whose staff shows up in person more often -- and whose chief executive became CEO of the combined business, Forvis. Some of the blended company's 5,400 total employees are now meeting new colleagues who could dictate future promotions and raises.\par \par \uc2 Sounds like a good time to get back to the desk.\par \par \uc2 "If you want to be a managing partner, you're probably not going to do that working one day a week in the office, and I think people get that," says Mr. Snow, who is now Forvis's chairman. Employees still can work from home much of the time, he notes, but there may be trade-offs.\par \par \uc2 Hybrid workers, beware: There can be a gap -- sometimes a wide one -- between what's required and what it really takes to succeed.\par \par \uc2 Office hard-liners like \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  have made clear that "a minimum of 40 hours in the office per week" is the only way to thrive, or even survive, at his company. The leaders of \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  and \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  also don't hide their disdain for remote work.\par \par \uc2 While telecommuting may be fine in certain roles, people in the upper ranks "cannot lead from behind a desk or in front of a screen," \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  CEO \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  wrote in his annual shareholder letter this spring.\par \par \uc2 Yet other businesses are promising "hybrid equity," insisting some employees can enjoy the conveniences of working from home without compromising their ambitions.\par \par \uc2 HubSpot\uc2 , a Boston-based digital marketing firm, plans to track promotions in the coming years to ensure people who rarely visit the office aren't disadvantaged, says \uc2 Katie Burke\uc2 , chief people officer. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  requires three days of office work per week, and human-resources head \uc2 Sara Wechter\uc2  says those who log only the minimum will have an "equitable opportunity to develop and advance their careers."\par \par \uc2 It's a dream for many workers, but it could be pure fantasy unless companies are vigilant, according to career coaches and researchers who say people in the office are more likely to get noticed and rewarded. A 2020 study of more than 400 tech workers by researchers at Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute and Northeastern University found that while remote and nonremote workers won roughly the same number of promotions, the salaries of remote workers grew more slowly. At companies where remote work was less common, telecommuters won fewer promotions.\par \par \uc2 Sure, you can hit your performance targets from the kitchen table and wear out the "raise hand" button on Zoom. But a colleague who chats up the boss when the meeting is over and goes for a drink after hours may get ahead.\par \par \uc2 There's a term for this.\par \par \uc2 Proximity bias (prak- si-M -te bi- s) | noun\par \par \uc2 1. A tendency to favor people in close proximity to you\par \par \uc2 2. Human nature and the way things have worked in business since forever\par \par \uc2 It's certainly possible to progress while working from home most or all of the time, especially in today's tight labor market, and not everyone aspires to climb the corporate ladder to the top. Still, hybrid and remote arrangements could be vulnerable to management changes or an economic downturn -- which many economists say is increasingly likely, by the way.\par \par \uc2 Businesses are hunting for leaders who can handle decentralized teams, says Bo Burch, founder of the executive-search firm Human Capital Solutions in Wilmington, N.C.\par \par \uc2 Yet, "companies aren't saying, 'Bo, you need to make sure you present a panel of executives that have great stories to tell about how they overcome proximity bias,' " he says.\par \par \uc2 Office-goers sometimes enjoy special status even at companies that have embraced remote work. \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and others have allowed many employees to scatter -- but warned of pay cuts for those who go remote and move to cheaper cities.\par \par \uc2 Polls show people in historically marginalized groups are among the most likely to prefer working from home, and businesses with hybrid teams should be careful not to exacerbate longstanding inequities, says Kathlyn Perez, a New Orleans labor lawyer who counsels companies on unconscious bias.\par \par \uc2 Then again, she notes remote workers aren't members of a legally protected class in the way that women, minorities and people with disabilities are. Those who feel that infrequent office visits unfairly cost them promotions could have little recourse.\par \par \uc2 "Unfortunately, if you know that your employer values some face time, then you as an individual trying to improve your working situation and endear yourself to your boss may want to put some of that face time in," she says.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Perez's advice might seem obvious. Not to everyone, apparently.\par \par \uc2 Overstock.com\uc2  CEO \uc2 Jonathan Johnson\uc2  expected good turnout, especially among young workers, when he extended a staff-wide invitation to join him for lunch every Tuesday at the company's Midvale, Utah, headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Total attendance over eight months: 10 people.\par \par \uc2 "Most of the time, I eat my peanut butter sandwich alone," he says. "When I was 25, if I had a chance to eat my sandwich with the CEO, I'd have been there."\par \par \uc2 He says he doesn't mind letting a majority of his 1,500 employees work from home most of the time, and Overstock recently hired executives in Austin and Cleveland to demonstrate its commitment to a hybrid workforce.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, when Mr. Johnson and I spent almost an hour chatting in a hotel lobby recently, I asked whether his lunchmates stand out as go-getters.\par \par \uc2 "A little bit," he allowed.\par \par \uc2 The man likes to talk in person. If I worked at Overstock and wanted to get ahead, I'd find out whether Mr. Johnson prefers Skippy or Jif and bring a jar to the office next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220609ei690001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
