\par\par \b \uc2 The New Workplace (A Special Report) --- Workers, Go Take a Hike: Having access to nature has benefits\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony C. Klotz \par \uc2 565 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 I was speaking recently with one of my former students about the decisions that leaders are facing as they bring employees back to the office on a full-time or hybrid basis.\par \par \uc2 This student said that one overlooked benefit of working from home is that it enables employees to access the outdoors during the workday. He described how his remote workdays included moments when he could go into his backyard and enjoy a nature break -- something he couldn't do at the office. He said these outdoor respites left him feeling more refreshed than his indoor breaks.\par \par \uc2 His observations aligned with my own research about the intersection of the worlds of work and nature, and with the compelling evidence that exposure to nature provides myriad benefits to individuals -- benefits that don't stop when the workday begins.\par \par \uc2 The link to the current discussion about remote and hybrid work is obvious: Employees who hold jobs that can be made hybrid -- namely office workers -- are the same workers who often have limited access to nature during their workdays in sealed buildings. Moreover, given that these buildings are often located in urban areas, even when employees are able to get outside, the quality of their exposure to nature is often low, occurring alongside distractions like traffic noise.\par \par \uc2 This is where flexible work comes in. When employees work from home, they can open their windows and breathe in fresh air. In between video calls, they can step outside and feel the breeze and hear birds. After lunch, they can take a walk to a nearby park, or work outdoors for a few hours. In short, in ways small and large, working remotely permits deeper immersion in nature compared with being in the office.\par \par \uc2 And this immersion matters. Contact with nature improves people's moods, sharpens people's cognitive abilities, makes them more cooperative, reduces burnout and enhances employees' productivity. By allowing workers more meaningful access to nature through flexible schedules, leaders are facilitating higher well-being and performance.\par \par \uc2 Companies, of course, could also redesign their in-person workspaces to provide employees with deeper immersion in nature -- something many firms are embracing by adding such things as green spaces on rooftops and hiking trails on corporate campuses. But such efforts are expensive. It is much less costly -- and quicker -- to incorporate remote work into employees' schedules.\par \par \uc2 A couple of caveats: First, individuals differ in how much they like the outdoors; investments in contact with nature will likely have little effect on employees who feel no connection to the natural world. In addition, however beneficial interactions with nature are, they can't make up for serious job deficiencies in things like fair pay and respectful treatment. It would ring hollow to tell overworked employees to simply go outdoors to avoid their impending burnout.\par \par \uc2 There are no one-size-fits-all answers when it comes to redesigning jobs in a postpandemic era. But when weighing the benefits of hybrid work and other flexible work arrangements, leaders shouldn't forget part of the answer can be found just outside their doors.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Dr. Klotz is the Anderson Clayton professor of business administration and an associate professor of management at Mays Business School, \uc2 Texas A&M University\uc2 . Email him at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220518ei5i00003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\